In article <36923F50.F8DED069@cyberdude.com>, Dani Steinmetz <danis@cyberdude.com> writes:
>Is there a way to do a read on multiple index keys ?  ex. multiple keys
>on an index file one for date, one for user name primary key is an
>incident number, can I do a read on user name = XXX, date = 12/21/98 and
>return the first record to match that criteria ?

If the file already exists and has keys that include just the individual
fields mentioned (primary is incident number only, secondary is user
name only, secondary is date only) then you can't do it as a simple
RMS call.

You can present _one_ key field and select _one_ key of reference for
an RMS indexed lookup (which is what Fortran is giving you an interface
to).  You cannot present a combination of more than one key value
for more than one key of reference.

If your data types and/or record offsets are chosen carefully, you may be
able to make a key that selects from more than data field.  For instance,
you could have an 8 character incident number in columns 1-8, a 20
character user name in 9-28 and an 8 character date in 29-36.  Then
if you had a 36 character RMS key you could look up

	...KEYID=0, KEY='0000000005VAXS09              01/05/99'...

You can also lookup leading substrings.  e.g.

	...KEYID=0, KEY='0000000005VAXS09              '...

For character keys, you can do non-contiguous keys that pick from
a field here and a field there.

Incompabitle data types could get in the way of this.  But if you
don't care about the collating sequence, you could just tell RMS
that it's one big character string anyway.

If you do care about collating sequence, you could leave your existing
keys alone and add a big segmented, character string key consisting
of all of your individual key fields.

If you cannot change the existing key layout then your next best option
is to do an indexed read selecting the most specific field comparison
available (date, username, incident number or whatever), compare the
other fields to decide whether you have a hit or a miss, and keep
reading sequentially and comparing until the value in the key field
changes.

You want to key into most specific field so that you check the fewest
possible candidate records.  If possible, you want to key by primary
index because sequential reads by primary key are faster than sequential
reads by alternate key.

Obligatory warning for first-time indexed file users:

	Record insertion into indexed files is slow.  The system has to
	update all the indexes.  And that's a lot of disk writes that
	may not be cached effectively.  Not a big deal for interactive
	access but for batch updates...

	If you're doing batch inserts that constitute a substantial
	fraction of the file (say 10 percent on up) you can get big
	performance wins by converting to a sequential file, adding
	the records there and then using $ CONVERT to create or
	re-create the indexed file.

	We're talking factors of 20 to 1 here.  And you get a nice
	clean file when you're done.

